h an approval on another head.\n\n### Identity And Scope\nSole native independent reviewer, not Rawls (proof author) or Hubble (integrator), and not an author-context fork. Previous reviews concerned different PRs. Model and effort were inherited; no lane or descendant was launched. MAIN attested sole ownership, no numeric CPA lane marker, caps0/0/0 and no competing writer. Those operational attestations are distinguished from this reviewer's direct Git/source/API checks.\n\nSole review worktree: /home/drx/MIPStarRE-qpbt/.worktrees/issue-116-expanded-line-status. Pinned local/public head: 397486e1986cb70acc9fbb5105294e3b6b0c8ba2. Ordered parents: d0f422693fed8865d713cc9a37a953a8c004e026 and 5738354a3ba7466fe19a33cf75f8006b2e5ba73c. Actual merge base remains 5738354a3ba7466fe19a33cf75f8006b2e5ba73c. Reviewed the complete four-path diff, not just the merge commit: 267 additions / 15 deletions across LineMeasurement.lean, LineMeasurement/Expanded.lean, audits/2026-09-15-issue116-expanded-line-status.md and chapter14_qpbt_observables.tex.\n\n### Freshness Correction\nMAIN's successful API/freshness preflight was against published5738354a. GitHub's PR568 merge occurred at 16:35:05Z as 29f0c16c737a5069a0b178d49e877416c5fa5824, BEFORE this task's 16:35:35Z admission, not necessarily during review. Thus 397486e1 was NOT fresh at admission. The first reviewer API read at 16:36:46Z confirmed main29f0c16c and head_is_fresh=false; later published main is ce787695f74026f1de29e33063b40e5d4f5900c3. MAIN explicitly authorized continuing this read-only pinned-head review and corrected the optimistic admission assumption. It did not waive merge freshness or authorize carry.\n\nThe exact-head source/CI/audit evidence remains valid for the reviewed head. This reviewer did not refresh, rebase, edit or retarget it. A different delegate must integrate actual published main; new CI and truthful canonical-patch identity are required before any later-head carry/publication. No such future evidence is fabricated here. Final API/local checks are recorded in /tmp/pr569-397486e1-final-context.json.\n\n### Statement-Integrity Audit\n1. Paper assumptions: chapter14:160-172 fixes an admissible tuple and a bipartite strategy winning with probability at least 1-epsilon. After Naimark dilation, projectivity is the standing convention. Numerical admissibility is the paper's field-size condition and m dividing q (chapter08:958-961); natural numbers are positive (chapter04:4-6). The source does not require strategy symmetry, equal player dimensions, traciality, nonzero line direction, positive/small epsilon or an extra error-estimate producer.\n2. Lean assumptions: AdmissibleParams supplies q,m,d, d>=1, admissible field size and m|q with a fixed scalar model. ProjectiveSetting consists of the actual Strategy, projectivity and winning inequality. Its distinct finite player spaces and unit state remain intact. Placement.IsOpposite enumerates exactly AA'->BA'', BA''->AA', BB'->AB'', AB''->BB'. These are faithful finite-coordinate and positive-natural boundary encodings. Nonnegativity of epsilon is derived from value<=1 and winning, not added as a premise. No bridge/residual/producer input occurs.\n3. Source conclusion: lem:qld-comm-line-cons has one polynomial error function for projectivity, self-consistency and two point-consistency relations, including symmetric equivalents. The proof's eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons-eps and following sentence assert the stronger linear-error item I for the concrete convolution. The distance definition at chapter06:258-271 is the expected full answer sum of squared state-vector norms bounded by O(error), with a hidden universal coefficient. It is not an unsquared distance or an exact coefficient-one claim.\n4. New Lean conclusion: expLine_point_cons_linear at LineMeasurement.lean:188 proves the actual opFamilyDistSq between each placed expanded line effect and its product with the selected opposite point effect, at most C*epsilon on psiHat. The existential C>=1 precedes all parameters, strategies, errors, placements and bases. There is no weakened answer sum or different error variable. The new blueprint node at chapter14:1916 displays exactly this bound and quantifier independence. Verdict: exact blueprint/Lean agreement; stronger than the common-error theorem in the small-error regime, and matching the separate source-proof assertion under the explicitly retained completion conventions.\n5. Proof and constants: obtain universal C1,C2>=1 from the forward and reversed low-degree winning theorems; choose C=2(C1+C2). For each directed placement, projective refinement gives D_I<=D_II; the Pauli overlap identity and agreement inequality give D_II<=2 Cons_strategy; the relevant winning theorem bounds Cons_strategy by Ci*epsilon. Epsilon>=0 gives the final constant enlargement. The reversed theorem concerns Alice's point/Bob's line on the original state, not an assumed strategy symmetry. All four cases were inspected. There is no derivation of epsilon from sqrt(epsilon), division by epsilon or hidden small-error range. The independent zero-error specialization compiled for arbitrary opposite placements. Verdict: genuine linear estimate, including the zero boundary.\n6. Law and answers: linePointDist is the unchanged one-half mixture of the axis and diagonal CL push-forwards. Its carrier retains the existing seed-bearing descriptions described in chapter13:163-178; forgetting the extra axis seed gives the source law. No conditioning, direction exclusion, sample reweighting or product-law replacement occurs. opFamilyDistSq sums every DegPoly(m*d) answer; axis outcomes outside the degree-d subspace have the existing zero effects. evalOpt returns a value only when well determined; expPointEffectAtLineAnswer is zero at none but does not discard that polynomial's line-effect contribution to the distance. The intermediate evaluation-class alphabet includes bottom, and the point measurement's bottom effect is zero. Verdict: exact existing completed-evaluation convention, explicitly retained; not a claim to erase the paper's documented evaluation discrepancy.\n7. Common-error theorem: existing expLine_point_cons, expLine_point_cons', exists_deltaLine and the intervening code are byte-identical to the base. The original deltaLine=sqrt(epsilon), IsPolyErr and ExpandedLineConclusions remain intact. The blueprint now correctly separates this common-error theorem from its stronger proof assertion. The corrected polynomial convention uses independent prefactor and positive exponent; its existing gap note is unchanged. Verdict: no weakening or hypothesis change to the source-labelled common theorem.\n8. Dependency/prose accuracy: both relevant proof blocks now include lem:qld-reversed-consistency, linked to the exact reversed theorem invoked. The source proof sketch is explicitly distinguished from the formal order of estimates. Actual proofs handle each directed bipartition without relying on the unfinished general symmetric-equivalent transfer lemma. The finite-sum Kronecker bilinearity comment matches the helper's identity and its direct completeness use. Verdict: accurate mathematics and certification; no new source-status inflation.\n9. Retained limits: existing completion/source-law and numerical qualifications are not newly discharged. The broader issue116 and general symmetric-equivalent transfer work remain; the public footer is Addresses #116. Source Lemma 4.13, #509/#510/#511 and inherited F11/#570 remain open/restricted as applicable. Incoming source-status material is preserved byte-for-byte, not re-audited or approved wholesale. No PR568/571 repair work was undertaken.\n\n### Verification And Historical Evidence\nRead the contract completely, four trusted primary prompts, AGENTS and substantive conventions; read the primary paper before the changed blueprint and proof. Reviewed every changed hunk and the directly reused refinement, overlap, winning, sampling, placeme