his statement, albeit with a slightly weaker quantitative bound.\n   652\t\n   653\t\\begin{proposition}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\simeq$\"]\\label{prop:simeq-triangle-inequality}\n   654\t\t\tSuppose that $A$, $B$, $C$, and~$D$ are measurements such that\n   655\t\t\t\\begin{equation*}\n   656\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot I \\simeq_{\\eps} I \\ot B^x_a,\n   657\t\t\t\\quad\n   658\t\t\tC^x_a \\ot I \\simeq_{\\delta} I \\ot B^x_a,\n   659\t\t\t\\quad\n   660\t\t\tC^x_a \\ot I \\simeq_{\\gamma} I \\ot D^x_a.\n   661\t\t\t\\end{equation*}\n   662\t\t\tThen\n   663\t\t\t\\begin{equation*}\n   664\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot I \\simeq_{\\eps + 2\\sqrt{\\delta + \\gamma}} I \\ot D^x_a.\n   665\t\t\t\\end{equation*}\n   666\t\t\t\\end{proposition}\n   667\t\\begin{proof}\n   668\tBecause $B$, $C$, and~$D$ are measurements, \\Cref{prop:simeq-to-approx} implies that\n   669\t\\begin{equation*}\n   670\tC^x_a \\ot I \\approx_{2\\delta} I \\ot B^x_a,\n   671\t\\quad\n   672\tC^x_a \\ot I \\approx_{2\\gamma} I \\ot D^x_a.\n   673\t\\end{equation*}\n   674\tThe triangle inequality, \\Cref{prop:triangle-inequality-for-approx_delta}, then implies that\n   675\t\\begin{equation*}\n   676\tI \\ot B^x_a \\approx_{4 \\delta + 4 \\gamma} I \\ot D^x_a.\n   677\t\\end{equation*}\n   678\tFinally, \\Cref{prop:triangle-sub} implies that\n   679\t\\begin{equation*}\n   680\tA^x_a \\ot I\n   681\t\\simeq_{\\eps + \\sqrt{4 \\delta + 4 \\gamma}} I \\ot D^x_a.\n   682\t\\end{equation*}\n   683\tThis completes the proof.\n   684\t\\end{proof}\n   685\t\n   215\t\\subsection{Distance measures}\n   216\t\n   217\tWe introduce several distance measures that are used throughout.\n   218\t\n   219\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between states]\n   220\t  \\label{def:state-distance}\n   221\t\n   222\t\tLet $\\{\\ket{\\psi_n}\\}_{n\\in \\N}$ and $\\{\\ket{\\psi'_n}\\}_{n\\in \\N}$ be two\n   223\t  families of states in the same space $\\mH$.\n   224\t  For some function $\\delta : \\N \\to [0,1]$ we say that $\\{ \\ket{\\psi_n} \\}$ and\n   225\t  $\\{ \\ket{\\psi'_n} \\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-close}, denoted as $\\ket{\\psi}\n   226\t  \\approx_\\delta \\ket{\\psi'}$, if $ \\norm{\\ket{\\psi_n} - \\ket{\\psi'_n}}^2 =\n   227\t  O(\\delta(n))$.\n   228\t  (For convenience we generally leave the dependence of the states and $\\delta$\n   229\t  on the indexing parameter $n$ implicit, writing e.g.\\ $\\ket{\\psi}$ for $\\ket{\\psi_n}$.)\n   230\t\\end{definition}\n   231\t\n   232\t\\begin{definition}[Consistency between POVMs]\n   233\t  \\label{def:consistency}\n   234\t\tLet $\\mX$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mX$.\n   235\t  Let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\cal{H}_A \\otimes \\cal{H}_B$ be a quantum state, and for\n   236\t  all $x\\in \\mX$, $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ POVMs.\n   237\t  We write\n   238\t  \\begin{equation*}\n   239\t    A^x_a \\otimes I_\\bob \\simeq_\\delta I_\\alice \\otimes B^x_a\n   240\t  \\end{equation*}\n   241\t  on state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and distribution $\\mu$ if\n   242\t  \\begin{equation*}\n   243\t    \\E_{x\\sim \\mu} \\sum_{a\\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\otimes B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}\n   244\t    = O(\\delta)\\;.\\footnote{Here the use of the $O(\\cdot)$ notation refers to the fact that the notation will often be used to measure consistency for \\emph{famillies} of POVM and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n\\in \\mathbb{N}$, which will always be clear from context but omitted for legibility, see Definition~\\ref{def:state-distance}. The $O(\\cdot)$ is taken as $n\\to\\infty$.}\n   245\t  \\end{equation*}\n   246\t  In this case, we say that $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are\n   247\t  \\emph{$\\delta$-consistent} on $\\ket{\\psi}$.\n   248\t\\end{definition}\n   249\t\n   250\tNote that a consistent measurement on $\\ket{\\psi}$ according to\n   251\tDefinition~\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same $\\ket{\\psi}$ and under\n   252\tthe singleton distribution, according to Definition~\\ref{d